ed for that like maybe summoned  players will be able to witness the  fight as they wait for their chance to  break out of the shield  that's a cool thought the Maronite  stands at about the halfway point in the  game so that might give you an idea of  the difficulty curve it's interesting to  note that in an interview with polygon  the game's director said that he was  confused by the fears of the North  American and European audience and he  didn't really understand them he has  continually repeated his goal to improve  the features there at the core of the  souls game and one of those things is  enemy AI so let's  about that in the demo many enemies  convincingly fake death they wait for  you to pass before waking up and they  don't make a whole lot of noise about it  and this is the sort of trick you'd  expect from a Souls game but while this  new behavior is interesting there seems  to be more of a broader subtler change  to enemy AI that makes me more excited  so this was reported by a few testers  simply put enemies seem smarter they no  longer just rush you initially and a few  people made it sound like they sized you  up just like you sort of size them up  when you learn how they behave moves  that were once easy to engage and  sidestep are no longer as easy a lunge  attack for example will adjust with your  movement and track your sidestep gone  are the days of the easy back step since  enemies will pivot and track your  movement much faster so this is great  because back steps were always a really  sort of failsafe way of taking out  humanoid enemies in Dark Souls so it's  great to see that gimped a little bit  test has also noticed that enemies made  better use of their dodge ability now  I've mentioned a few times now that  enemies are sort of really situationally  aware and this is a recurring theme  enemies will push their advantage  a few play testers noticed that once  they sort of backed up to heal during a  fight that enemies wouldn't just keep  t